From b13d1fb71e501b09ee88c05d24d4c3f415e2d6a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dan Brickley <danbri@danbri.org>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 13:58:49 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] mod_mix_pam: recognize supported MIX namespaces

Problem and evidence:

bounce_sm_packet used only xmpp:has_subtag(Msg, #mix{}). The bundled xmpp codec compares both the element name and namespace, so that predicate matches the empty namespace on locally constructed mod_mix records but does not match decoded records carrying explicit Core:0 or Core:1 namespaces. Runtime tracing confirmed both representations are relevant at the participant-server boundary.

Protocol expectation:

A participant server implementing XEP-0405 must forward messages from a joined MIX channel. ejabberd and its bundled codec support XEP-0369 Core:0 and Core:1, while mod_mix currently routes locally built MIX records before an encode/decode round trip.

Change:

Keep the original empty-namespace predicate and add explicit Core:0 and Core:1 predicates through a small helper. Export the helper only in TEST builds and cover all three supported representations.

Status-quo compatibility:

The original locally generated empty-namespace representation remains accepted. The patch adds decoded Core:0 and Core:1 records without changing emitted stanzas, channel mappings, persistence, cache behavior, MAM behavior, or resource routing. Unrelated and unknown namespaces remain rejected.

Interoperability and operational risks:

The patch activates the existing all-active-resources delivery path for explicitly namespaced MIX messages, which may reveal separate duplicate-handling bugs in clients with multiple resources. Runtime cost is at most two additional subtag predicates in this narrow groupchat hook. Reverting this commit restores the single original predicate.

Verification:

Focused EUnit on Erlang/OTP 29.0.5 accepts the local empty representation, Core:0 and populated Core:1; rejects an unknown MIX namespace and a non-MIX subtag; 5 tests, 0 failures. The complete image builds on pinned Erlang/OTP 28.5.0.4. An authenticated loopback client joined through PAM, received its unique message live, and recovered it through channel MAM. A Tailnet WebSocket browser displayed the server echo immediately, and a distinct BeagleIM 6.0.1 account delivered a live message to the browser. Exact multi-resource delivery count, no-mapping rejection, and a wire-level Core:0 proof remain tracked before upstream consideration.
